<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Pius IX</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Giovanni Maria Mastai-Ferretti</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Pius IX reigned from 1846 to 1878, the longest pontificate in history. Driven briefly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by the revolution of 1848, he lost most of the Papal States to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1860 and Rome itself in 1870, after which he called himself a prisoner in the Vatican. He convened the First Vatican Council, which defined papal infallibility.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix.jpg" alt="Pope Pius IX" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pius_IX,_by_Adolphe_Braun,_1875.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
